{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793808786,"nonce":"d6FObDb4J8mJQEhmtjWCo5+Dcy0=","message":"The full interpreter-first sweep exposes the same region-quality problem in op_overload. Frozen NUC binaries: old whole-function path wall median 29.694 ms; interpreter-first 186.177 ms (3 thermally gated samples per arm, ~6.27x). Correct result 210000000 in both. mandelbrot also regresses 34.663 -\u003e 52.122 ms in this sweep. These are preliminary performance samples, not claimed improvements.\n\nNUC op-hir/fn-lua_main_3-body.hir has a scalar loop (125 total dump lines), while fn-lua_main_3_resume0-outlined-opt.hir has 571 lines, retains receiver/metatable guards, generic zl_arith fallbacks and the two per-iteration result table allocations. The region receives the existing objects as pointer parameters; the constructor facts visible to whole-function optimization are absent. Trace confirms interpreted entry, request after 16 loop visits, and Cranelift transfer. A separate diagnostic using an LD_PRELOAD getenv shim for ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE, preserving snapshot eligibility, also confirms an actual later LLVM transfer into the outlined region. This is not simply a frame stranded in the interpreter or a missing LLVM promotion. Investigate receiver refinements and partial escape across OSR boundaries; do not replace the interpreter-first policy with a blocking first-call compile.\n\nFull sweep: all supported Lua/Python kernel checksums match. Python samples were one per arm, so small differences are not reliable performance claims. No source changes were made after the correctness corpus and benchmark build. Evidence archive /private/tmp/zyntax-interp-first/nuc-evidence.tar.gz, SHA-256 6f5b0f6f3ea8c450e60d86876f7cbc9a6029792daf5ca1a087272cef82f74faf, contains raw samples, scripts, hashes, thermal records and NUC IR/traces. Existing source provenance is recorded in source-hashes.json in the parent directory.","files":null}]}